<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palvancha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Paloncha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palvancha is an industrial town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadradri-kothagudem-district">Bhadradri Kothagudem district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, near the Kinnerasani river. It was the seat of the Paloncha samasthan, a hereditary estate in the forested Godavari valley. Today it is known for the Kothagudem Thermal Power Station and other industries that draw on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palvancha.jpg" alt="Palvancha" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Iharishs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KTPS_at_Palvancha_03_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Palvancha was part of a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> estate known under the Qutub shahi dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> as the Hasanabad Paragana, and later as the Palvancha Zamindari in the Nizam's Dominion; the estate's name was given by Captain Glasfurd. The zamindar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a> was also zamindar of Palvancha. Under Abdul Hasan Tana Shah, the Qutubshahi ruler, the post of tahsildar of Palwancha, the official who collected the land revenue, was held by Kancharla Gopanna, the saint and composer known as Bhakta Ramadasu.</p><p>Gopanna's maternal uncles held high positions at the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>. He is remembered for his devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and for building the Sita Ramachandraswamy Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadrachalam">Bhadrachalam</a>, which lies in the Palwancha area. The NDC and KLR Degree and PG College carries out archaeological research on megalithic sites in the region.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Palvancha is a twin town of Kothagudem in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhadradri-kothagudem-district">Bhadradri Kothagudem district</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>, and an industrial area with the Kothagudem Thermal Power Station, a works of the NMDC and a TSIIC industrial park. It was long an independent municipality of 39 wards; in late 2025 the Telangana government merged it with Kothagudem to form the Kothagudem Municipal Corporation. National Highway 30 passes through the town, and its nearest railway station is Kothagudem, also called Bhadrachalam Road.</p><p>The University College of Engineering of Kakatiya University stands on the outskirts. Nearby are the Kinnerasani Wildlife Sanctuary and the Kinnerasani dam, where the Telangana Tourism Development Corporation has sanctioned a boat-riding facility. Besides the festivals kept across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, its people celebrate the regional festivals of Telangana, among them Bonalu and Batukamma.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palwancha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paloncha</b> was a hereditary estate in the forested country of the Godavari valley, near Bhadrachalam, held by a Telugu chiefly family.</blockquote><a href="/state/paloncha-samasthan">Paloncha Samasthan</a></li></ul></section>
